    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "therapy feels like a joke when there's nothing left to fix. it's like people expect me to \"rebuild\" something that doesn't exist anymore. the athlete is dead. that part of me is just finished. it's not like a bad breakup where you move on, it's like the main character of the story just stopped existing and now i'm just reading the epilogue of a book i don't even want to be in.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's completely understandable why therapy might feel inadequate when the core of who you were feels irrevocably gone. The process isn't always about \"rebuilding,\" but sometimes it's about learning how to *inhabit* this new space without carrying the weight of the old self. Instead of trying to find the athlete, perhaps therapy can help you explore what a meaningful life looks like for the person who is here right now. Give yourself permission to grieve the end of that chapter rather than forcing yourself toward an imagined continuation.",
      "source": "target",
